        "description": "Conjecture: For a natural family of theorem statements {T_n} that admit canonical local restrictions to overlapping subinstances (for example graph, number-theoretic, or simplicial families), there exists a computable presheaf of partial proofs such that T_n has a short global proof if and only if the associated first proof-cohomology obstruction group vanishes. Test: Define the proof presheaf for at least one concrete family with explicit overlaps, compute the obstruction classes for small n, and compare vanishing/nonvanishing against actual minimal proof lengths in a fixed formal system; the conjecture is supported if low proof complexity coincides with vanishing obstruction and hard instances exhibit persistent nontrivial classes. Refutation occurs if one finds an infinite family where obstruction vanishes uniformly but proof length still grows superpolynomially, or nonvanishing appears despite uniformly short proofs. Impact: This would introduce a genuinely new topological invariant of proof complexity, turning global proof synthesis into a gluing problem and potentially enabling obstruction-based lower bounds, modular proof search, and new interfaces between homological algebra, automated reasoning, and complexity theory.",
