        "title": "p-adic Gradient Descent Periodicity Predicts Generalization in Modular Learning",
        "description": "Conjecture: For families of neural networks trained on arithmetic tasks over finite fields F_p (such as modular addition, inversion, or low-degree polynomial evaluation), if the update rule is lifted to an integer dynamical system and analyzed modulo p^k, then there exists a task-dependent critical exponent k_0 such that eventual periodicity of the training trajectory modulo p^k for all k \\le k_0 is equivalent to uniform out-of-distribution generalization across infinitely many primes p in the family. Test: Train such networks across many primes and tasks, record parameter trajectories under gradient descent, reduce them modulo p^k, and check whether stable low-period orbit structure up to level k_0 correlates exactly with cross-prime generalization; a single systematic counterexample refutes the conjecture. Impact: This would introduce a new arithmetic-dynamical invariant of learning, connecting optimization, number theory, and representation behavior, and could yield a predictive theory of when neural networks truly learn algorithms rather than interpolate.",
