        "title": "Spectral Universality of Proof-State Transition Graphs Across Formal Domains",
        "description": "Conjecture: For large families of proof-state transition graphs generated from distinct theorem domains (e.g. algebra, analysis, combinatorics) under a fixed prover and resource budget, the normalized bulk eigenvalue distribution of the non-backtracking operator converges to a domain-independent law after conditioning on degree sequence and graph size. Test: Construct proof-state graphs from multiple formal libraries, compute the non-backtracking spectra, and compare them against degree-preserving null models and across domains using two-sample distribution tests; the conjecture is confirmed if cross-domain distances shrink with graph size while remaining separated from null-model artifacts, and refuted if spectral laws remain domain-specific. Impact: This would suggest a hidden universality class for mathematical search dynamics, enabling transfer of proof heuristics, synthetic benchmark generation, and principled complexity diagnostics for automated theorem proving.",
