        "id": "fd_0392",
        "title": "Homological Obstructions to Finite Completion in Knuth-Bendix Systems",
        "description": "Conjecture: For every finite terminating first-order rewrite system R, construct its critical-pair overlap complex O(R) whose vertices are rewrite rules and whose 2-cells encode minimal critical-pair diamonds. Then there exists a computable integer invariant beta_1(O(R)) such that if beta_1(O(R)) = 0, Knuth-Bendix completion terminates with a finite convergent system whenever completion succeeds on all rule-induced subtheories of size at most 3; conversely, there exists an infinite family with beta_1(O(R)) > 0 for which every fair completion procedure generates infinitely many new rules. Test: Define O(R) explicitly, compute beta_1 on benchmark rewrite systems, and compare against actual completion behavior; confirmation requires strong correlation plus provable positive/negative families, while a counterexample with beta_1(O(R)) = 0 and locally successful subtheories but unavoidable infinite completion refutes it. Impact: This would give a topological predictor for completion success, connect rewriting to combinatorial topology, and enable new certified heuristics for symbolic algebra and equality saturation.",
