        "id": "fd_0395",
        "title": "Proof-Theoretic Phase Transitions from p-adic Valuation Dynamics in Integer Line",
        "description": "Conjecture: For families of integer matrices arising as boundary operators, syzygy matrices, or certificate systems in formalized mathematics, there exists a universal valuation-instability statistic V_p(A) built from the growth of p-adic valuations of minors under Schur complements / elimination steps such that sharp jumps in proof search hardness occur exactly near primes p where V_p(A) crosses a critical threshold. Test: Compute V_p(A) across many benchmark families (persistent homology complexes, resolution-derived matrices, algebraic certificate matrices, theorem-library dependency linearizations) and compare against measured hardness of prime-sensitive tasks (mod-p rank computation, torsion extraction, certificate search, refutation search). The conjecture is refuted if no statistically significant critical-threshold phenomenon appears, or if hardness is explained equally well without valuation dynamics. Impact: This would link arithmetic geometry, proof complexity, and computational topology through a new mechanism for predicting when specific primes make reasoning suddenly hard, enabling prime-adaptive algorithms and a new arithmetic theory of computational phase transitions.",
