        "id": "fd_0405",
        "title": "Mod-p Betti Resonance Predicts Integer Torsion Exponents",
        "description": "Conjecture: For every finite CW complex X of dimension at most d with cellular boundary matrices of entry norm at most M, there exists a universal function F_d such that for each prime p and each homological degree k, the multiset of p-primary torsion exponents in H_k(X; Z) is determined up to total error at most F_d(log M) by the persistence profile of the mod-p Betti numbers of the p-adic thickenings of X obtained by replacing each cellular attaching map with its reductions modulo p^r for 1 <= r <= R, once R exceeds the largest p-torsion exponent. Test: Compute complexes with known integer homology, build the p^r-reduction tower, measure whether the sequence of mod-p barcode jumps reconstructs the exact p-primary invariant factors; a single infinite family with bounded dimension and growing torsion where reconstruction systematically fails refutes the conjecture. Impact: This would create a new bridge from computable mod-p persistence data to full integral homology, giving an unexpected route to recovering torsion structure from field-level invariants and potentially yielding scalable algorithms for arithmetic topological data analysis.",
